Z||000005||filthyscarecrow||11-07-2001||01:48 AM||||i worked for trek for a while. <BR>don't get a dual susp bike. they soak up too much of your power when you go up hills. just get a standard hardtail bike and get a suspension seatpost. they're like $100 (rock shox). the 8500LT is an awesome bike. they also have a softtail that's all carbon fiber adn uses the flex in the bottom bracket as a pivot. it's called the STP. i rode the STP400 for a weekedn and it wasn't like any full susp bike. very nice. tha's what i know..||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||11-07-2001||01:25 PM||||You want a good dually?  Specialized M5 FSR.. 23.5 pounds, race ready.  Looks sweeeet.  <P>I didn't like full suspension until I actually got one (Kona U'hu) that I use for urban assault and just general goofing around.  4 inches of travel front and rear is more than enough for me (right now anyways).  My other ride is an Independent Fabrications Deluxe.  Expensive, but worth it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Neal||11-08-2001||02:00 AM||||More of just a local thing but <A HREF="http://www.pinkbike.com" TARGET=_blank>www.pinkbike.com</A>  has just some crazy stunts, about a week back a local guy rode off a bill board platform, 37 FOOT DROP the pics were just sick, then i drove past the bill board even crazier than the pics.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Neal||11-08-2001||02:08 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/benny.jpg"> <P><BR>go to the photos and search "benny" youll get the rest of them.||||reg||1
Z||000009||sloth85GT||11-08-2001||02:27 AM||||I disagree with the above mentioned comments made pertaining to going with a hartail vs. a dualie. I ride FS (full suspension) and it's great, really comfy. But I ride hard, and a lot, I'm also over 200lbs, so the suspension helps in keeps my rear wheel planted through the bumps. <P>You don't feel 5 more pounds of wieght, I used to ride a 23 pound hartail, and now I ride a 30 pound full suspension bike, and you know what? I'm never going back. BUT, that's not to say that FS is for everyone, if all you are going to use your bike for is city commuting on paved streets, go with a hartail and low travel front suspension to minimize the bob. If you are going to be pounding the dirt like I do, go FS. <P>I've got a 2000 Giant Warp DS-1 with about 4.5" of travel in the rear and a Rock Shox Psylo XC tuned for 5" up front, that's a little extreme, but my sag is set to 1.5" up front and 1" in the back so it's not so bad. <P>Truly it all depends on your needs. And if you go with an FS bike, you have to get a good one, pay at least $800US for it. There's lots of el cheapo dual suspension bikes out there.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.mts.net/~kuzma/fieronl.gif">      <BR>Dan<BR>1985 Silver GT<p>[This message has been edited by sloth85GT (edited 11-08-2001).]||||reg||1
